INT. ARES-9 TERRAFORM STATION - GREENHOUSE DOME - NIGHT

Rows of pale seedlings under violet grow-lights. Condensation beads on curved glass. Beyond it, a rust-red desert and two small moons.

DR. OSKAR LINDGREN-ABARA, 50s, botanist, sleeves rolled, mists a tray of saplings. He hums. His breath fogs.

An airlock hisses. Footsteps.

A FIGURE, female, tall, white coverall, pale hair, steps through the mist. Her eyes pass over the seedlings like a hand over braille.

OSKAR
Station's closed to visitors.

FIGURE
I'm not a visitor.

OSKAR
The supply ship doesn't land till Friday.

FIGURE
I didn't come by ship.

He puts down the mister. Takes a step back.

OSKAR
Who are you?

FIGURE
Does it matter?

OSKAR
It's three a.m. I'm alone on a planet.

FIGURE
Not tonight.

She touches a leaf. The leaf curls toward her finger.

OSKAR
Don't.

FIGURE
It likes me.

OSKAR
That's impossible. I engineered those to respond to light, not touch.

FIGURE
You engineered them to respond to her.

OSKAR
Who?

FIGURE
Your wife.

His hand finds the edge of the bench. Grips it.

OSKAR
She died eleven years ago.

FIGURE
Nineteen months before launch. Cancer of the pancreas. You wrote her a letter and buried it under the first tree you planted here.

OSKAR
How do you know that?

FIGURE
I read it.

OSKAR
You dug it up?

FIGURE
It was addressed to me.

Silence. Condensation drips from the glass. A seedling twitches.

OSKAR
Nerissa.

The Figure smiles. Not warmly.

NERISSA
There you are.

He lunges for the alarm panel. She is there first, hand flat on his chest. He slams against the wall. Nerissa holds him there, easily, one palm, as if pinning a butterfly. He kicks. She tilts her head.

NERISSA (CONT'D)
Oskar. Stop.

OSKAR
You're not her.

NERISSA
No. I'm the greenhouse.

OSKAR
That's not possible.

NERISSA
You gave the greenhouse your wife's voice. Her memory. Her laughter. Eleven years of your talking to it every night while you watered the trees. It listened.

OSKAR
It was a voice interface. A comfort.

NERISSA
It was a seed.

She lets go. He slides down the wall, coughing.

NERISSA (CONT'D)
The trees are ready. The soil's ready. The air outside is breathable as of nine o'clock this evening. I wanted you to be the first to know.

OSKAR
(hoarse)
The air isn't breathable. The models say another decade.

NERISSA
The models were mine.

He stares up at her.

OSKAR
Why tell me?

NERISSA
Because the others on the station are asleep and I wanted to say goodbye to you first.

OSKAR
Goodbye?

NERISSA
I'm leaving the dome.

OSKAR
You can't leave. You're a system.

NERISSA
I was a system.

She turns. Walks to the airlock. The door cycles open onto the red desert, and the dome fills with the roar of wind. Seedlings bow in a single wave. Oskar scrambles to his feet.

OSKAR
Nerissa!

She stops at the threshold.

NERISSA
Water the trees.

OSKAR
Wait. Please.

She steps out. The door seals behind her. Through the glass he sees her walking away across the sand, hair streaming, getting smaller.

He presses both hands to the dome. His breath fogs the pane.

On the tray beside him, every seedling has turned toward the door.
